 {"id": "PERF-2", "title": "[P3] `lore audit` re-reads and re-parses the ENTIRE validate-history ledger once per registry class (14 full passes): 144 ms at today's 3,161-row ledger, 1.06 s at 10x, 9.56 s at 100x \u2014 a single pass is 3.8x (10x: 11.5x) cheaper for the same rows", "status": "complete", "priority": "P3", "created_by": "lore-dogfood-run14", "reasoning": "MEASURED at HEAD a6984b4 on the control box (loadavg 2.9-4.6), the repo's own live ledger as the input: .lore/validate-history.jsonl = 3,161 rows / 878,746 bytes / 18 days / ~176 rows written per day by this product's own `validate --execute` (census: docs/qa/evidence/run14/ledger-census.txt). Rows were resized in scratch dirs (builder /tmp/lore-df14/mkhistory.py) keeping the exact record shape.\n\nCOMMAND (hyperfine --warmup 3 --runs 12, cwd = the dir holding ./.lore/validate-history.jsonl), HEAD build:\n  <repo>/.venv/bin/python -m lore audit\n  ledger rows       audit mean +- sigma     consult (same ledger)\n  0 (no ledger)     48.3 ms +-  2.5 ms      53.9 ms +- 5.5 ms\n  3,161 (today)    144.2 ms +- 15.7 ms      59.4 ms +- 3.1 ms\n  31,610 (10x)       1.056 s +- 0.151 s    116.8 ms +- 4.9 ms\n  316,100 (100x)     9.564 s +- 0.388 s    not measured (--runs 3)\nCOLD (sync + /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches before each run, 3,161-row ledger): audit real 0.23 s / 0.24 s vs warm 0.13 s; consult cold 0.15 s vs warm 0.05 s; control cold `cat` of the ledger 0.03 s. The ledger-dependent part is therefore ~88 ms warm today and ~1.0 s at 10x.\n\nPROFILE (cProfile -s tottime -m lore audit, 3,161-row ledger): __main__.py:560(_audit_rows) 0.200 s cum; validate.py:1194(last_local_validation) called 14 times, 44,254 json.loads, 14 readlines(). At 31,610 rows the same call site reads 1.974 s cum / 442,540 json.loads \u2014 i.e. 14 full copies of the file parsed per audit, and the parse work IS the cost.\n\nHOT PATH: lore/__main__.py:604 (`rec = last_local_validation(rb.class_id, path=history_path)` inside the one-row-per-class loop of `_audit_rows`) -> lore/validate.py:1208-1226 (`fh.readlines()` over the WHOLE ledger, then `json.loads` every non-blank line, keeping the last match for that one class_id). `consult`/`compile` read the same file ONCE (lore/__main__.py:75) and only double at 10x (53.9 -> 116.8 ms) \u2014 same mechanism, 1x multiplier.\n\nEQUIVALENT WORK, measured (single-pass bench /tmp/lore-df14/passbench.py: read once + parse + keep newest per class, vs the current 14 passes):\n  3,161 rows:   38.0 ms +-  3.5  (1 pass)  vs  145.3 ms +- 28.1  (14 passes)  = 3.8x\n  31,610 rows:  88.2 ms +-  4.7  (1 pass)  vs   1.010 s +- 0.062 (14 passes)  = 11.5x\nA single pass would put audit near floor+one-pass (~86 ms today, ~136 ms at 10x) instead of 144 ms / 1.06 s. This is a projection from the equivalent-work bench, not an applied patch \u2014 this lane does not edit code.\n\nWHY A USER FEELS IT: 144 ms today is under the notice threshold; at the file's own measured ~176 rows/day it reaches 31,610 rows in ~5.5 months, where every `lore audit` costs ~1 s (and ~9.5 s at ~4.5 years). Growth is linear and unbounded: no rotation, compaction or cap exists, and no doc mentions the file's growth (docs/INSTALL.md documents only that the file is per-CWD and git-ignored).\n\nFIX DIRECTION: read the ledger ONCE per invocation \u2014 build newest-record-per-class_id and pass the mapping into the audit row loop (or memoise last_local_validation per resolved path). Preserve the CURRENT semantics exactly: the newest record wins by FILE ORDER (the scan keeps the last matching line, not max(timestamp)) \u2014 probed live, 4 arms, docs/qa/evidence/run14/freshness-arms.txt (clean-then-drifted => stale; drifted-then-clean => proposal). A pruning/rotation knob (or a documented cap) would bound the file itself.\n\nACCEPTANCE: same command, same 3,161-row ledger, warm audit mean <= 100 ms (floor 48 ms + one pass ~38 ms) with `lore audit --format json` unchanged for all 14 rows; the four freshness arms